        Modern coding agents write code that runs. What they miss is what you <em>meant</em> — the agent assumed one thing, you had another in mind, and the next session starts over with no memory of why. LID closes that gap: you put the design down in plain English, the agent compiles specs, tests, and code from it — and when something's off, you fix the design once and everything downstream rewrites itself to match.
      </p>

